1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
A few people asked me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defense or getting completely rolled or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl where there's barely anywhere to move.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,000
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

3
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:15,160
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore.

4
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:18,440
Because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well.

5
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:25,180
Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

6
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,300
But the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched,

7
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

8
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit, using chip damage,

9
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:46,900
sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on, and waiting for someone to

10
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:50,740
overextend instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned.

11
00:00:50,740 --> 00:00:55,420
Because even when an opening looks really good, you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

12
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:59,480
whether anyone can actually help you, and whether dying for that play is even worth it.

13
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:05,960
Especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack defend, because you can't take your life for granted like Blue Team Cat, because

14
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:09,900
those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous, and

15
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:14,120
one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

16
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:20,180
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows. You can make half the enemy team turn around,

17
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:24,080
You can go around, pull someone away from the objective, or force their medic backwards,

18
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:27,620
but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

19
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,860
So we're going to go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm going to

20
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:34,780
lightly commentate, and how to stay useful when you can't hold W, how to know when to

21
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:39,760
commit, and how to tell when you made a bad play versus when your team just didn't take

22
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:42,500
advantage of what you created.

23
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,380
In downtime situations like this on defence, chip damage is sometimes all you can really

24
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:48,600
do, but it's more important than people think.

25
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,300
It makes people look back, slows them down and forces them to look for heals.

26
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:56,160
And make sure not just when pestering people, but in general, make sure you're on an elevated

27
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:58,660
platform when holding any position with scout.

28
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:01,820
It's crucial that if you get the chance, to not let engineers get their sentry built in

29
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:02,900
the first place.

30
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,840
If it's level 3, you alert your team and when your combo comes out to deal with it, help

31
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:10,640
them if you can at that very moment, but otherwise pay attention to the flanks.

32
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,280
The engineer was on the ball here, and he starts building again so I call it out to

33
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:18,360
my soldier friend and he deals with it straight away.

34
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,760
The engineer's probably thinking, oh my god I'm so unlucky, they keep walking out as soon as I build my stuff.

35
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:29,060
But it isn't luck, I'm scouting out these areas, I'm being an actual scout, and I'm communicating what I see to my team.

36
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:45,460
At the same time I not overextending I treating these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2 I just taking it slow taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we playing defense

37
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking. You don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing Scout.

38
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:07,640
Right here I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout.

39
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,480
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

40
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,720
I run up it, jump backwards and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward

41
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,060
over the pyro's head.

42
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:20,920
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed and just happens to set me up to kill

43
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,140
the scout as well.

44
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:25,640
That's one of the best skills you can have as a scout, not just seamlessly using anything

45
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:29,1000
around you, but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

46
00:03:29,1000 --> 00:03:33,640
It's a bit like Emmet becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're

47
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:39,400
looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking, can I use it as high ground?

48
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,1000
Basically any type of elevated platform will let you get extra height and reposition with

49
00:03:42,1000 --> 00:03:46,280
less risk.

50
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,780
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding

51
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,460
a boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

52
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:55,480
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

53
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:59,080
Scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention,

54
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:00,600
without just throwing his life away.

55
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:04,600
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and

56
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:16,860
drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

57
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:20,360
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend, and the enemy

58
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:24,280
has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

59
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,600
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

60
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,620
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially

61
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,920
when you're on defence.

62
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,180
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them

63
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:41,380
down and keep running time off the clock, but you need to be alive to do this.

64
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:45,360
Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

65
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,840
red is more about survivability.

66
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:57,360
The previous clip was a good example of what scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area, but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

67
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:20,599
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focused in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med and clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with and safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push

68
00:05:20,819 --> 00:05:26,599
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without overcommitting and throwing away the position your team has already established.

69
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:51,639
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout and narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

70
00:05:51,639 --> 00:05:55,419
Honestly, maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

71
00:05:55,419 --> 00:06:00,339
the most seriously. They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly,

72
00:06:00,339 --> 00:06:05,159
Sadly, especially for red, your respawn can be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds and the

73
00:06:05,159 --> 00:06:07,579
points can be captured absurdly quickly.

74
00:06:07,579 --> 00:06:10,919
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly

75
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:11,919
makes scout amazing.

76
00:06:11,919 --> 00:06:15,739
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights

77
00:06:15,739 --> 00:06:18,799
you take and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

78
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:22,459
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe.

79
00:06:22,459 --> 00:06:25,779
Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to

80
00:06:25,779 --> 00:06:27,319
snipers and spam.

81
00:06:27,319 --> 00:06:31,479
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point and be prepared to back up once

82
00:06:31,479 --> 00:06:33,719
your team loses that space.

83
00:06:33,719 --> 00:06:37,819
In this clip I move around and help my team defend the choke, but I always end up returning

84
00:06:37,819 --> 00:06:39,819
to the same flank besides the point.

85
00:06:39,819 --> 00:06:43,879
It gives me cover, close range fights and an easy escape, while still allowing me to

86
00:06:43,879 --> 00:06:45,379
directly defend the point.

87
00:06:45,379 --> 00:06:49,019
It's also a really common place for blue to put a teleporter, so it's naturally somewhere

88
00:06:49,019 --> 00:06:51,079
scout can find a lot of value.

89
00:06:51,079 --> 00:06:52,779
Target priority is also important.

90
00:06:52,779 --> 00:06:56,679
In general, if you see something like a direct hit soldier, there's a good chance he's

91
00:06:56,679 --> 00:06:58,679
I'm not using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

92
00:06:59,119 --> 00:07:04,439
Scout could choose his fights, so you should use that freedom to focus the people who are most threatening to your team.

93
00:07:04,539 --> 00:07:08,439
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dust Bowl.

94
00:07:08,679 --> 00:07:14,339
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory and getting someone's attention and then immediately retreating.

95
00:07:14,819 --> 00:07:19,319
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far, which brings them directly into your team.

96
00:07:19,319 --> 00:07:24,119
That's what happens to this Pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then and my soldier friend already

97
00:07:24,119 --> 00:07:28,079
I already was holding that position behind me and I knew that so he deals with the pirate straight away.

98
00:07:28,079 --> 00:07:33,079
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the pirate into a fight that's much better for my team.

99
00:07:33,079 --> 00:07:47,639
And give Coldouts a scout because I already mentioned this but he cold scout for a reason and you often the first person who can see what Blue is doing So make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about It doesn need to be complicated

100
00:07:48,379 --> 00:07:50,919
You know, it's just that their combo is coming through the top left flank

101
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:54,659
or a pyro is jetpacking towards you or someone is moving into your team space.

102
00:07:54,779 --> 00:07:58,679
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalize.

103
00:08:00,439 --> 00:08:04,799
Now, I know not everyone wants to treat casual like a scrim and constantly give out cold outs,

104
00:08:04,799 --> 00:08:10,919
But this 6's clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are and how powerful Scalper comes when his team can actually

105
